ct predates the Cretaceous–Paleogene boundary by roughly 300,000 years: the crater is either synchronous with the boundary or some 300,000 years older than it, and it cannot be both. IODP–ICDP Expedition 364 sank a borehole into the Chicxulub peak ring in 2016, an operation that produced a core of shocked granite and suevite from the peak ring. Deep drilling into the crater structure thus supplied the physical material against which the competing accounts of the extinction could be tested, and it is on such direct sampling, rather than on argument from correlation alone, that the judgement of the geological community finally rested."
